body:
Oh, well, I found out what the difference is. To cut it short, it comes to the fact that I have a parent object, that contains the one, that I dispose.

So, to reproduce the bug, you may take the same webgl_test_memory example, and change these lines:

```
var mesh = new THREE.Mesh( geometry, material );
scene.add( mesh );
renderer.render( scene, camera );
scene.remove( mesh );
```

to these lines:

```
var mesh = new THREE.Mesh( geometry, material );
var object3D = new THREE.Object3D();
object3D.add(mesh);
scene.add( object3D );
renderer.render( scene, camera );
scene.remove( object3D );
```

This helps:

```
object3D.remove( mesh );
scene.remove( object3D );
```

So if I remove the child of object3D before removing the object3D itself, then the memory leak goes away.
